---
import { nextViewCoreSlides, nextViewAppendixCategories } from '../../../data/pitchDeckData';
---

      <article id="article-9" class="article-block">
        <!-- Article 9 Header -->
        <div class="article-header">
          <div class="article-category-badge">
            <span class="badge-dot"></span>
            <span>ARTICLE 09 &bull; CONVERSATIONAL SEED DECK &bull; NEXTVIEW VENTURES</span>
          </div>

          <h2 class="article-title">
            NextView Ventures: The Conversational Pitch Deck Framework
          </h2>

          <p class="article-lead">
            NextView Ventures, a prominent seed-stage venture capital firm, deliberately redesigned the standard pitch deck to fix a major flaw in fundraising: the rigid, monologue-style "flipbook" presentation. Their framework is built to foster a genuine, two-way conversation that builds trust and puts the founder on equal footing with the investor.
          </p>

          <div class="article-meta-bar">
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Reading time:</strong> 8 min read</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Firm:</strong> NextView Ventures</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Stage:</strong> Seed Stage</span>
            <span class="meta-divider">&bull;</span>
            <span class="meta-item"><strong>Architecture:</strong> 5-8 Core Slides + "Kitchen Sink" Appendix</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Two-Part Architecture (Core Deck vs Kitchen Sink) -->
        <section class="yc-philosophy-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">CORE PHILOSOPHY</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">TWO-PART ARCHITECTURE</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">The Conversational Architecture</h3>
          <p class="slide-intro">
            To achieve a genuine, interactive dialogue rather than an awkward one-way lecture, NextView splits the presentation into two complementary artifacts:
          </p>

          <div class="philosophy-cards-grid">
            <div class="philosophy-card">
              <div class="ph-card-icon">01</div>
              <h4 class="ph-card-title">Part 1: Core Discussion Deck</h4>
              <p class="ph-card-desc">
                5 to 8 condensed slides delivered in under 5 minutes. Hits the highlights quickly so the investor grasps the gist immediately, opening the room for natural conversation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="philosophy-card">
              <div class="ph-card-icon">02</div>
              <h4 class="ph-card-title">Part 2: "Kitchen Sink" Appendix</h4>
              <p class="ph-card-desc">
                A massive backup library containing all deep financial models, granular GTM funnels, product roadmaps, and anticipated FAQ objection defenses.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="philosophy-card">
              <div class="ph-card-icon">03</div>
              <h4 class="ph-card-title">The "Equal Footing" Heuristic</h4>
              <p class="ph-card-desc">
                Moving away from theatrical pitching places founder and VC side-by-side as collaborative problem solvers analyzing a compelling business together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 2: Part 1 - The Core Discussion Deck (5 to 8 Slides) -->
        <section class="slides-overview-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">PART 1 &bull; 5-8 SLIDES</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">CORE DISCUSSION DECK</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">Part 1: The Core Discussion Deck</h3>
          <p class="slide-intro">
            The goal of the core deck is to hit the highlights quickly and succinctly so the investor grasps the gist of the business in just a few minutes, leaving the rest of the meeting open for a natural dialogue:
          </p>

          <!-- Quick Navigation Bar for Article 9 -->
          <nav class="toc-nav" aria-label="NextView slide quick links">
            <span class="toc-label">Jump to slide:</span>
            <div class="toc-pills">
              {nextViewCoreSlides.map((slide) => (
                <a href={`#nv-slide-${parseInt(slide.num)}`} class="toc-pill">
                  {slide.num}. {slide.title}
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </nav>

          <!-- Summary Cards Grid -->
          <div class="summary-cards-grid">
            {nextViewCoreSlides.map((slide) => (
              <a href={`#nv-slide-${parseInt(slide.num)}`} class="summary-card">
                <div class="card-num-row">
                  <span class="slide-badge">SLIDE {slide.num}</span>
                  <span class="slide-question">{slide.question}</span>
                </div>
                <h3 class="card-slide-title">{slide.title}</h3>
                <p class="card-slide-desc">{slide.desc}</p>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="section-divider" />

        <!-- Core Slide Deep Dives (1 to 8) -->
        <div class="key-points-list">
          
          <!-- Slide 1: Intro -->
          <div id="nv-slide-1" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">01</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 1</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">1. Intro</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              A brief opening slide to <strong>set the stage and introduce the company name</strong>. Keep it clean and immediately transition to the team.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 2: Team -->
          <div id="nv-slide-2" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">02</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 2 &bull; UPFRONT PLACEMENT</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">2. Team (Upfront)</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              <strong>NextView recommends placing the team slide immediately at the beginning to establish credibility upfront.</strong>
            </p>
            <p class="point-text">
              They note that this slide should be highly straightforward, though founders frequently over-engineer it. Highlight who you are, relevant past achievements, and why you are uniquely qualified.
            </p>
            <div class="callout-tip">
              <strong>The Upfront Advantage:</strong> Placing the team on Slide 2 anchors the investor's perspective. When they know who is building the company, the rest of the product and market slides carry vastly higher credibility.
            </div>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 3: What do you do? -->
          <div id="nv-slide-3" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">03</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 3</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">3. What do you do?</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              This slide should explain your product using a <strong>simple 1-2 sentence statement</strong>.
            </p>
            <p class="point-text">
              NextView advises against being overly clever; instead, <strong>use visual cues like product screenshots, customer logos, or a simple workflow diagram</strong> so the investor immediately understands the physical or digital reality of the product.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 4: Is it working? (Traction) -->
          <div id="nv-slide-4" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">04</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 4</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">4. Is it working? (Traction)</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Share the <strong>highest-level view of your early growth, customer acquisition, or product milestones</strong> to prove that the concept has momentum. Avoid getting bogged down in granular cohorts here—save detailed cuts for the appendix.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 5: Why does it matter? (Market) -->
          <div id="nv-slide-5" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">05</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 5</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">5. Why does it matter? (Market)</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Define the market problem clearly. NextView recommends <strong>articulating this market problem in one simple sentence</strong>, ideally using <strong>one "killer stat" or a powerful analogy</strong> that makes the customer's pain point undeniably obvious.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 6: Can you be best in the world? (Metrics) -->
          <div id="nv-slide-6" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">06</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 6</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">6. Can you be best in the world? (Metrics)</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              Highlight the <strong>core product advantages, growth drivers, or financial metrics</strong> that demonstrate why your company will beat competitors and capture the market.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 7: Where are you going? -->
          <div id="nv-slide-7" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">07</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 7</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">7. Where are you going?</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              A <strong>high-level look at the vision and where the company will be in the coming years</strong>. Paint the picture of what success looks like when your wedge expands into category leadership.
            </p>
          </div>

          <hr class="section-divider" />

          <!-- Slide 8: What do you want? (The Ask) -->
          <div id="nv-slide-8" class="slide-deep-dive">
            <div class="slide-tag-row">
              <span class="slide-number-pill">08</span>
              <span class="slide-tag-title">NEXTVIEW CORE SLIDE 8</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="slide-heading">8. What do you want? (The Ask)</h3>
            <p class="point-text">
              <strong>State the amount of capital you are raising</strong> to conclude the formal introduction and immediately transition into interactive conversation.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <hr class="section-divider" />

        <!-- Section 3: Part 2 - The "Kitchen Sink" Appendix -->
        <section class="kitchen-sink-section">
          <div class="section-tag-row">
            <span class="section-badge-pill">PART 2 &bull; BACKUP LIBRARY</span>
            <span class="section-tag-name">THE SECRET WEAPON</span>
          </div>

          <h3 class="slide-heading">Part 2: The "Kitchen Sink" Appendix</h3>
          <p class="slide-intro">
            The <strong>"Kitchen Sink" is the secret weapon of the NextView framework</strong>. Because the core deck is intentionally brief, founders must come armed with a massive, highly detailed appendix that acts as a backup library.
          </p>
          <p class="point-text">
            This appendix contains all the dense, complex slides that normally bog down a pitch:
          </p>

          <div class="philosophy-cards-grid">
            {nextViewAppendixCategories.map((cat) => (
              <div class="philosophy-card">
                <div class="ph-card-icon">{cat.num}</div>
                <h4 class="ph-card-title">{cat.title}</h4>
                <p class="ph-card-desc">{cat.desc}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <!-- How it Works in the Meeting Callout -->
          <div class="callout-conclusion" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <strong>How it works in the meeting:</strong> Instead of forcing the investor to sit through 20 detailed slides, you deliver the 5-minute core deck and open the floor. If an investor asks a deep question—for example, about your specific go-to-market strategy—you simply say, <em>"Great question, let me pull up page 6 in the appendix,"</em> and use that detailed slide as a backdrop for the discussion.
            <br /><br />
            This format proves to the investor that you have a <strong>masterful, data-backed handle on every detail of your business</strong>, while allowing the meeting to naturally follow the investor's specific areas of interest.
          </div>
        </section>

        
      </article>
